<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parting of the Red Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Crossing of the Red Sea, Kriat Yam Suph</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> is an episode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> were pursued by the Egyptian army; at God's command Moses held out his staff, the waters of the Yam Suph parted, and the Israelites crossed on dry ground. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> followed, the waters returned and drowned them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> also relates the crossing, and Muslims fast two days in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> to commemorate it. No archaeological evidence directly supports the account apart from the biblical text, and scholars debate where the crossing took place, since Yam Suph is literally a sea or lake of reeds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parting-of-the-red-sea.jpg" alt="Parting of the Red Sea" width="330" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Poussin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Crossing_of_The_Red_Sea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plagues-of-egypt">Plagues of Egypt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> agrees to let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> go, and they travel from Ramesses to Succoth. Ramesses is generally identified with modern Qantir, the site of the 19th dynasty capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, and Succoth with Tell el-Maskhuta in Wadi Tumilat. Led by a pillar of cloud by day and of fire by night, they go to Etham, where God tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to turn back and camp by the sea at Pi-HaHiroth. None of the later places named has been identified with certainty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>God causes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> to pursue them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> are afraid when they see the army, but the pillar separates the two camps. At God's command <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> holds out his staff, the waters part, and the Israelites walk through on dry land with a wall of water on either side. At daybreak God clogs the chariot wheels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and throws them into a panic, and when Moses lets the sea close, the pharaoh and his army are destroyed. The Israelites then sing a song of praise. According to Soggin's documentary hypothesis the narrative has at least three and possibly four layers, in the oldest of which God drives the sea back with a strong east wind.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> also tells of the crossing. It says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> was told to strike the sea with his staff, and that each part of the divided sea was like a huge mountain. Muslims fast on two days in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a> to commemorate the event.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The theme was taken up by the panegyrists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> and applied to the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in 312. It was popular on carved sarcophagi in the fourth century, and at least twenty-nine survive in full or in part. Eusebius of Caesarea cast Maxentius, drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>.</p><p>No archaeological evidence directly supports the story apart from the biblical account. Zahi Hawass, formerly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Minister of State for Antiquities Affairs, has called it a myth. The Hebrew Yam Suph means &quot;sea of reeds&quot;; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>'s Greek translators rendered it Erythraean, &quot;Red&quot;, Sea. Proposed sites include Lake Timsah, the Isthmus of Suez, the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> delta and Nuweiba Beach in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>. Natural explanations proposed for the event include a volcanic eruption, a wind setdown of the water and a mirage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parting of the Red Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Talmud explains the shorter Passover Hallel by the Temple sacrifice, which was the same each day of the festival; other sources say the Full Hallel is left out on the seventh day in consideration of the Egyptians who died at the <b>crossing of the Red Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li></ul></section>
